| 1 | what-happened | On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court agreed to decide whether the government must hold, while their removal cases are pending, people who are living in the United States and were never admitted. The order takes the case; it does not decide it. Two laws are in play. One, called here the detention statute, says that in the case of an applicant for admission, a person seeking admission "shall be detained" if an officer determines that person is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, except for a crewman, a stowaway, or a person an earlier provision of the detention statute already covers, and except that a person arriving by land from a contiguous territory may be returned there instead. The other, the bond statute, says the government "may release" a person on bond. | scotus-order-list-2026-10-01: THURSDAY, OCTOBER 1, 2026; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-order-list-2026-10-01: RHONEY, ACTING DIR. V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA, RICARDO A.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-order-list-2026-10-01: The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01: Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01.txt<br>uscode-2024-1225: in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained; data/sources/immigration-detention/uscode-2024-title8-1225.txt<br>uscode-2024-1225: who is a crewman; data/sources/immigration-detention/uscode-2024-title8-1225.txt<br>uscode-2024-1225: to whom paragraph (1) 