| 5 | what-happened | The Second Circuit held that Barbosa da Cunha may be an applicant for admission but is not seeking admission, because he is not requesting lawful entry after inspection and authorization; he was already living here. So the court held that the detention statute does not apply to someone in the United States after entering without inspection and admission who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter. The Second Circuit affirmed the district court, which had required a bond hearing or release. Under the July 10 memorandum, an applicant for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Parole is a separate power. The parole statute lets the government release a person "only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit". The July 10 memorandum says the only people eligible for release on bond are people already admitted. | ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: because he is not requesting lawful entry into the United States after inspection and authorization; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not apply to noncitizens, such as Petitioner, who are present in the United States after entering the country without inspection and admission; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: may not be released from DHS custody except by INA § 212(d)(5) parole.; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: The only aliens eligible for a custody determination and release on recognizance, bond, or conditional parole under INA § 236(a) are aliens admitted to the United States; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt |
| 6 | what-happened | Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.” | ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt |
| 7 | what-happened | Barbosa da Cunha entered around 2005, lived in the United States for more than twenty years, applied for asylum in 2016, and held a work permit. He has never been arrested for or charged with a crime. If the government wins, people in his situation cannot be released on bond: the Second Circuit wrote that if the detention statute applies, he "must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond". By September 10 the question had reached every numbered federal appeals court. Nine, including the Second and the Fourth, are the courts the Fourth Circuit said had held that the bond statute applies to noncitizens inside the country, like the people who filed the cases, and that they must be given a bond hearing. The Second Circuit's own holding covers people who entered without inspection and were not apprehended while entering or shortly afterward. Two, the Fifth and the Eighth, held that the detention statute applies to everyone who has not been admitted. | ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: lived in the United States for more than twenty years after entering the country; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: applied for asylum in 2016 and was granted a valid work permit.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: has never been arrested for or charged with a crime.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: Decided: September 10, 2026; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: The First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits concluded that Section 1226 applies to noncitizens inside our country; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: like the Petitioners, and that they must thus be provided a bond hearing.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: who are present in the United States after entering the country without inspection and admission; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: The Fifth and Eighth Circuits ruled, on the other hand, that Section 1225 applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted to the United States; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: they must be detained without bond; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: Today we join the overwhelming majority of circuit courts of appeal that have considered this question; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt |
