| 16 | where-this-sits | On March 6, 1997, a Federal Register rule, effective April 1, said people present without having been admitted or paroled would be eligible for bond. The Second Circuit counts Executive Branch practice over thirty years and across five administrations, and it says the Justice Department understood the detention statute the same way when it issued that rule. On September 25, 2026, the full Second Circuit refused to rehear the case. Judge Steven J. Menashi dissented, arguing that the detention statute applies and that Barbosa da Cunha must be detained without a bond hearing. Judge Matthew A. Schwartz voted against rehearing while writing that he agreed with the dissent's analysis. | fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06: Thursday, March 6, 1997; data/sources/immigration-detention/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06.txt<br>fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06: Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.; data/sources/immigration-detention/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06.txt<br>fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06: Effective date: This interim rule is effective April 1, 1997.; data/sources/immigration-detention/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Executive Branch practice over thirty years; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: across five Presidential administrations; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: the Department of Justice’s contemporaneous understanding of the statute was the same as Petitioner’s; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25: 09/25/2026; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25.txt<br>ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25: rehearing in banc is hereby DENI