2 INTRODUCTION In the decision below, the Second Circuit determined that 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A), which mandates the detention without bond hearings of certain aliens while their removal proceedings unfold, does not apply to aliens who are present in the United States for some time after having entered the country unlawfully. The Second Circuit’s decision conflicts with decisions of the Fifth and Eighth Circuits, both of which have held that aliens present without admission are subject to Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s detention mandate. In Raycraft v. LopezCampos, petition for cert. pending, No. 25-1415 (filed June 22, 2026), the government filed a petition for a writ of certiorari asking this Court to resolve that conflict. The Court should hold the petition in this case pending the resolution of the petition in Lopez-Campos and then dispose of this petition as appropriate. STATEMENT A. Legal Background The Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), 8 U.S.C. 1101 et seq., authorizes the removal of certain classes of aliens from the United States. See 8 U.S.C. 1182, 1227. Various provisions of the INA further authorize the detention of certain aliens pending their removal proceedings. See, e.g., 8 U.S.C. 1225, 1226. This case concerns several INA provisions addressing removal and detention that were enacted in 1996 as part of the Illegal Immigration Reform and Immigrant Responsibility Act (IIRIRA), Pub. L. No. 104-208, Div. C, 110 Stat. 3009-546. 1. Before 1996, the applicable procedures for detaining and expelling an alien under the INA depended largely on whether the alien had physically “entered” the United States, regardless of whether that entry had been lawful. “[E]xpelling an alien who had already en-
LINE 30 0 chars

LINE 45 0 chars

LINE 46 15 chars
--- page 15 ---
LINE 47 1766 chars
8 INA’s text. See Todd M. Lyons, Acting Director of U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, Interim Guidance Regarding Detention Authority for Applicants for Admission (July 8, 2025). DHS concluded that “applicants for admission” are “subject to detention under [Section 1225(b)] and may not be released from ICE custody” except by “parole” under Section 1182(d)(5). Ibid. (emphasis omitted). As a result, except for aliens subject to Section 1226(c), the “only aliens eligible for a custody determination and release on recognizance, bond, or other conditions under [Section 1226(a)] are aliens admitted to the United States and chargeable with deportability under [Section 1227].” Ibid. (emphasis omitted). The Board of Immigration Appeals reached the same conclusion in a published opinion. In re Yajure Hurtado, 29 I. & N. Dec. 216 (2025). B. Factual And Procedural Background 1. Respondent is a native and citizen of Brazil who entered the United States illegally around 2004 or 2005 and has remained in the country since then. App., infra, 7a. In September 2025, ICE officers encountered respondent at a traffic stop in Norwood, Massachusetts, determined that he had entered the country without inspection or admission, and took respondent into custody. Id. at 7a, 73a. DHS officers initiated removal proceedings against respondent, charging him with being inadmissible under 8 U.S.C. 1182(a)(6)(A)(i), for being present in the United States without being admitted or paroled, and being inadmissible under 8 U.S.C. 1182(a)(7)(A)(i)(I), for not possessing valid immigration documents. App., infra, 8a. DHS determined that respondent must be detained for the duration of his removal proceedings pursuant to Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s detention mandate. Respondent
LINE 48 0 chars

