src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:50:        <p data-layer="narrative">For about thirty years, by the Second Circuit's account, people who entered without inspection and were not admitted or paroled, and who were not apprehended while entering or shortly afterward or held on a criminal or terrorism ground, could ask for bond. On July 10, 2025, a memorandum from the commissioner of Customs and Border Protection said the detention statute covers them instead. On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals said immigration judges lack authority to grant them bond. On September 26, 2025, officers arrested Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts; an immigration judge denied him a bond hearing, a district court ordered a bond hearing or release within ten days, and an immigration judge released him on bond after finding no danger and no flight risk. On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit ruled the other way from the Board in Barbosa da Cunha's case. On October 1 the Supreme Court agreed to decide the question.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Executive Branch practice over thirty years" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="without inspection and admission" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="detain noncitizens on certain criminal or terrorism-related grounds" /><Cite s="fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06" passage="aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="in July 2025, the government changed that long-settled practice" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18" passage="on July 10, 2025" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="FROM: Rodney S. Scott" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="section 235 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), rather than section 236, is the applicable immigration detention authority for all applicants for admission." /><Cite s="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" passage="Immigration Judges lack authority to hear bond requests or to grant bond to aliens who are present in the United States without admission." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Petitioner-Appellee Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="arrested Petitioner while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Petitioner requested a bond hearing, which an immigration judge denied based on a determination that he was subject to mandatory detention" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="The district court granted the petition and ordered the government to either provide a bond hearing or release Petitioner within ten days." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="presents no danger to persons or property or risk of flight, released him on bond." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus." /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." /></p>
src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:118:          <p>The signed memorandum, a one-page scan, is addressed from Commissioner Rodney S. Scott. It says the department's position is that applicants for admission are subject to mandatory detention and may not be released except by parole.<Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="FROM: Rodney S. Scott" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="SUBJECT: Detention of Applicants for Admission" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="subject to mandatory detention under INA" /></p>
src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:127:          <p>Customs and Border Protection's public notice says Commissioner Rodney S. Scott issued guidance on July 10, 2025, and that applicants for admission encountering the department for the first time after failing to lawfully present for inspection at a port of entry are subject to mandatory detention.<Cite s="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18" passage="on July 10, 2025" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18" passage="subject to mandatory detention under INA Section 235" /></p>

